{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310700,"nonce":"L/L7+AEfGCW0ElK+aeQ27NfPPV4=","title":"ALL FOUR FIXED. Three tests printed wrong output yet scored PASS: Type-API class-literal id (626516a0), unsound class-\u003einterface cast ...","message":"Found by reading test_results.txt output that the harness scored green. All\nverified against code, all fixed. Suite went 180 -\u003e 181 passing (one new\nregression test) with zero spurious IMPORT diagnostics.\n\n\n`run_haxe_tests.sh` scores PASS unless the process exits non-zero OR prints a\nmarker at column 0. So a test can print `fail2`, or announce that an imported\nmodule will trap at runtime, and still be green. **A test with no assertions is\nnot a test** — several here only `trace()` values whose expected answers live in\ntrailing comments.\n\n\n`Type.getClassName(Animal)` → null, while\n`Type.getClassName(Type.getClass(dog))` → \"Dog\". A class used as a value lowered\nto its **raw SymbolId**, but object headers, TYPE_REGISTRY and\nCONSTRUCTOR_REGISTRY are keyed by the **deterministic FNV-1a name hash**. Every\n`Class\u003cT\u003e` literal was a guaranteed registry miss. Same disease family as the id\n-space cluster: SymbolIds are per-context.\n\nFix: the Class arm in variable lowering now calls `deterministic_class_type_id`\nwith the same fallback as `runtime_type_id` — mirroring the Enum arm directly\nbelow it, which was already correct.\n\nI first read the SIGSEGV that appeared afterwards as a createInstance defect.\nIt was not — see the one-arg `cast` entry below. `Type.createInstance` with a\nclass literal works.\n\n\n`test_interface_cast` prints `fail2`. Widget implements only IDrawable, yet\n`cast(w, IClickable)` returns non-null. Real Haxe 4.3.6 (verified with\n`--interp`) throws `Class cast error` — `cast(e,T)` is CHECKED; the\nnull-returning form is `Std.downcast`.\n\nRoot: in the class→interface safe-cast arm, `known_implements` is\n`interface_vtables.contains_key(\u0026(src,tgt)) || interface_method_names.contains_key(\u0026tgt)`.\nThe second disjunct asks *\"is the target interface known here?\"* — true for every\ninterface in the module — instead of *\"does the source class implement it?\"*.\nTwo amplifiers keep the bad value alive: the wrap failure path returns the RAW\nclass pointer instead of null, and the interface-wrap helper mints a\n`forward_ref_dispatch_thunk_by_name` stub whose body is `unreachable`, so the\nfat pointer looks complete. Calling `c.click()` would branch to `unreachable`.\nThe same loose predicate sits on the cross-module arm.\n\nFix: `interface_vtables` is the authority, but only once it has ANY entry for\nthat class (cross-module the pairs may never have been built); a class it knows\nnothing about keeps the permissive by-name path. The wrap-failure path returns\nnull instead of the raw class pointer.\n\n**MEASURED, do not repeat the mistake:** `SymbolTable::implements_interface`\nreturns FALSE even for relationships that DO hold, because `class_hierarchies`\nis not populated at MIR time. The obvious \"use the real implements test\" fix\ndoes nothing. Probe with `RAYZOR_CAST_DIAG`-style eprintln before trusting a\npredicate here.\n\nSeparately, the W0014 advisories in that test are a DIFFERENT defect (interface\nreturn type erased in HIR/TAST and recovered by name at MIR) and fixing one does\nnot affect the other.\n\n\n`test_safety_use_after_move` prints\n`error[IMPORT]: imported module 'Resource' failed to compile … Cannot find name '__resources__'`.\nThe file declares its **own** `class Resource` and never references\n`haxe.Resource`.\n\nRoot: dependency extraction inserts every bare uppercase type-path name as a\ndependency **without subtracting the file's own declarations**, then the import\nloader takes any dep with no dot and GUESSES a package from a fixed list\n(`haxe.iterators`, `haxe.ds`, `haxe`, `sys.thread`, `sys`, `haxe.exceptions`,\n`haxe.io`), taking the first that resolves. `haxe.Resource` resolves, so it\nloads — and fails, because `__resources__` is only defined when resources are\nembedded.\n\nThis is exactly what [[feedback_fqn_first_resolution]] forbids. Fixed by\nsubtracting the file's own classes/interfaces/enums/abstracts/typedefs from its\ndependency set. The loader still guesses for genuinely-undotted stdlib names;\nnarrowing that is separate.\n\n\n`var a:A = cast make();` crashed; `var a:A = cast(make(), A);` worked. The\none-arg form was typed **Dynamic**, so the binding stayed Dynamic and `a.name`\ntook the Dynamic receiver path, calling `haxe_unbox_reference_ptr` on a value\nthat was never boxed — it reads value_ptr at offset 8 with NO tag check, so on a\nraw object it returns field slot 1 and the load dereferences it.\n\nA box and an object are indistinguishable from memory alone (both start with a\nclass id), so the runtime cannot defend itself — the type must be right.\n\n`cast expr` now adopts the expected type from context, but **only for a class or\ninterface target**. Adopting an abstract routes it through @:from conversions,\nwhich broke `var arr:SafeArray = cast plain` in test_forward. Regression test:\n`test_onearg_cast_call_result.hx`.\n\nThis is what I first mis-blamed as a `Type.createInstance` defect — createInstance\nwas fine; the `cast` around it was not.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_silent_test_failures_2026_08_12` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}